          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The Kantorovich monad is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+monad"} probability monad
            on the category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+metric+spaces"} complete metric spaces
            and 1-Lipschitz (or
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lipschitz+map"} Lipschitz
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            the space of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Radon+measure"} Radon
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+measures"} probability measures
            with finite first moment, equipped with the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kantorovich-Wasserstein+distance"} Kantorovich-Wasserstein distance
          %p
            Its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+over+a+monad"} algebras
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+set"} closed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convex+set"} convex
            subsets of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Banach+spaces"} Banach spaces
          %p
            The construction was first given by van Breugel for the compact case (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#vanbreugel"} vB ‘05
          %p
            There is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/order+theory"} ordered
            version of the Kantorovich monad, making use of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stochastic+order"} stochastic order
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+over+a+monad"} algebras
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+set"} closed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convex+set"} convex
            subsets of
            = succeed "." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                ordered Banach spaces
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/ordered+Banach+spaces"} ?
          %h2#wasserstein_spaces Wasserstein spaces
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            be a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+space"} metric space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+measure"} probability measure
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            has
            %strong finite first moment
            if
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ×
                      %mi X
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi dp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi dp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo +
                  %mn ∞
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\int_{X\times X} d(x,y) \, dp(x)\,dp(y) \;\lt\; +\infty,
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d(-,-)
            denotes the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distance"} distance
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
            be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Radon+measure"} Radon
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+measures"} probability measures
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            of finite first moment. The
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kantorovich-Wasserstein+distance"} Kantorovich-Wasserstein distance
            is given by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi p
                  %mo ,
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≔
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %munder
                    %mi inf
                    %mrow
                      %mi r
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi p
                      %mo ,
                      %mi q
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ×
                      %mi X
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi dr
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  d(p,q) \;\coloneqq\; \inf_{r\in\Gamma(p,q)} \int_{X\times X} d(x,y) \,dr(x,y) ,
          %p
            where the infimum (which is attained if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is complete) is taken over the set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi p
                  %mo ,
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma(p,q)
            of
            %span.newWikiWord
              couplings
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/joint+measure"} ?
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\times X
            which admit
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
            as their
            = succeed "." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                marginals
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/marginal+measure"} ?
          %p Equivalently, the distance can be written as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi p
                  %mo ,
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %munder
                    %mi sup
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo ∈
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ∫
                  %mi f
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi dp
                  %mo −
                  %mo ∫
                  %mi f
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi dq
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  d(p,q) \;=\; \sup_{f\in[X,\mathbb{R}]} \int f\, dp - \int f\, dq,
          %p
            where the supremum (which is usually not attained) is taken over the set of 1-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lipschitz+map"} Lipschitz
            functions
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\to \mathbb{R}
            = succeed ".)" do
              %span.newWikiWord
                Kantorovich duality
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Kantorovich+duality"} ?
          %p
            The
            %strong 1-Wasserstein space
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is the space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P X
            of Radon probability measures on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            with finite first moment, equipped with the Wasserstein metric.
          %h3#properties Properties
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                is
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+metric+space"} complete
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi P
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P X
            %li
              %p
                If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                is
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/separable+space"} separable
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi P
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P X
            %li
              %p
                If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                is
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact"} compact
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi P
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P X
          %p
            More information on Wasserstein spaces can be found in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#villani"} Villani ‘08
          %h2#functor_unit_and_multiplication Functor, unit and multiplication
          %p
            As it is customary for
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+monads"} probability monads
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:X\to Y
            (in this case, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lipschitz+map"} Lipschitz
            or 1-Lipschitz map) the map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P f: P X \to P Y
            induced by the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pushforward+of+measures"} pushforward of measures
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P f
            is again Lipschitz, with the same Lipschitz constant as
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            on the category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+metric+spaces"} complete metric spaces
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lipschitz+maps"} Lipschitz maps
            (or 1-Lipschitz).
          %p
            Again, as it happens in general with
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+monads"} probability monads
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi δ
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta:X\to P X
            is given by forming the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+measures"} Dirac measures
            and the multiplication
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo :
                  %mi P
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E:P P X\to P X
            is given by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration"} integration
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ξ
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi P
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \xi\in P P X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mi ξ
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi PX
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E \xi\in PX
            is defined as the one which assigns to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Borel+set"} Borel set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\subseteq X
            the number
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mi ξ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≔
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ∫
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi d
                  %mi ξ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  E \xi(A) \;\coloneqq\; \int p(A) \,d\xi(p) .
          %p
            The maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            satisfy the usual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axioms"} axioms
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
            (see
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#kantorovich19"} F-P ‘19
            = succeed "." do
              %strong Kantorovich monad
          %h2#algebras Algebras
          %p
            As it is the case for most
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+monad"} probability monad
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convex+spaces"} convex spaces
            of a particular kind. For the Kantorovich monad, the objects of the underlying category are complete metric spaces, and so the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+set"} closed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convex+set"} convex
            subsets of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Banach+spaces"} Banach spaces
            are an ideal candidate: they are
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+spaces"} metric spaces
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+metric+space"} complete
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convex+space"} convex combination operation
          %p
            More in detail, every closed convex subset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            of a Banach space comes equipped with a map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi e
                  %mo :
                  %mi P
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e:P A\to A
            given by vector-valued
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration"} integration
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ↦
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ∫
                  %mi a
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi dp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  p\;\mapsto\; \int a \,dp(a) .
          %p
            The integral exists since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            has finite first moment. Conversely, it can be proven that every
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-Moore+category"} morphisms of algebras
            are maps between algebras
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:A\to B
            which commute with the integration map. It can be shown that equivalently these are exactly the
            %strong affine
            maps, i.e.~those maps which satisfy
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %mi λ
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi x
                  %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo −
                  %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi λ
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo −
                  %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  f\big(\lambda\,x + (1-\lambda)\,y\big) \;=\; \lambda\,f(x) + (1-\lambda)\,f(y).
          %p
            For more details, see
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#kantorovich19"} F-P ‘19
          %h2#duality Duality
          %p (…)
          %h2#the_ordered_case The ordered case
          %p
            (Work in progress. For now see
            = succeed ".)" do
